    <h1 class="pillar-h1">Medicare Explained: Your Complete 2026 Guide</h1>

    <!-- AEO Direct Answer Block -->
    <aside class="aeo-direct-answer" aria-label="Quick Answer" id="quick-answer">
      <p class="aeo-label">Quick Answer</p>
      <p class="aeo-text">
        Medicare is the federal health insurance program — established under Title XVIII of the Social Security Act — covering Americans 65 and older and younger people with qualifying disabilities. It has four parts: Part A (Hospital Insurance), Part B (Medical Insurance), Part C (Medicare Advantage — private bundled plans), and Part D (Prescription Drug Coverage). As of 2026, over 67 million Americans are enrolled, per the CMS Medicare and You 2026 handbook. Most people pay $0 for Part A and $202.90 per month for Part B.
      </p>
    </aside>

    <!-- Lead Paragraph -->
    <p class="article-introduction">
      Medicare is not complicated once you see the whole picture at once. What makes it confusing is that most sources explain only one piece at a time — premiums here, enrollment dates there, Medigap in a separate article. We built this guide, last reviewed in August 2026 by the Seniors Audit research team, to give you the full picture in one place: what each part covers, who qualifies, when to sign up, what it costs, and which traps to avoid. By the time you finish reading, you will know exactly what decisions you need to make.
    </p>

